# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
#
# WRITTEN BECAUSE THE GUESS WAS ALREADY WRONG HERE (#99). ci.yml produced six
# check runs; the settings page required five. The one left out was
# `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu`, and of the six it is the least redundant:
# this crate addresses a disk image by 64-bit offsets and cluster indices, and
# on a target where `usize` is 32 bits a `u64` narrowed with `as usize` keeps
# only its low 32 bits. Every other leg of the matrix runs on a 64-bit runner
# where that conversion is lossless, so the test for it is written
# `#[cfg(target_pointer_width = "32")]` and compiles out of all of them (#62).
# That job is the only one in the repository that can see a truncated offset,
# and until this file existed it could go red without stopping a merge.
#
# Names must match the check-run names GitHub reports, including the
# "test / <os>" job-matrix spelling with spaces around the slash, and the
# job's `name:` where it differs from its key (`test-32bit` reports as
# `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu`).
#
# Every name below runs on `pull_request` — ci.yml is the only workflow that
# does, release.yml triggers on a version tag — so each one can actually
# report on a PR. A required check that never reports is a permanent block,
# which is why release.yml's jobs are deliberately absent.
#
# THE SEVENTH NAME, AND THE ORDER IN WHICH IT BECOMES A GATE (#98).
#
# `test (release)` is added to ci.yml in the same pull request that adds this
# line. Until now every `cargo test` in this repository built the `test`
# profile, which inherits from `dev`; the crate published to crates.io is built
# with `[profile.release]`, and nothing ran a single test against it. It is not
# a duplicate of the matrix: release lets an integer overflow wrap where debug
# panics and drops every `debug_assert!` body, so a test guarding a wrapping
# computation passes the debug run for the wrong reason while the behaviour it
# guards against is live in the shipped library. The long comment above the job
# has the rest.
#
# THE SPELLING IS `test (release)` AND NOTHING ELSE. It comes from the job's
# `name:`, which is our own text: literal parentheses, a single space, and NO
# slash. `test / release` -- the matrix shape a few lines up -- is a name
# nothing will ever report, and with enforce_admins on that is a repository
# nobody can merge into.
#
# ORDERING. github-guard promotes a declared name to required only once it has
# been green on `main`, so declaring it here in the same commit that creates the
# job does not strand this pull request: the job reports, the pull request
# merges on the six names already required, and the seventh becomes a gate on
# the next reconciliation after `main` is green. That is the intended sequence,
# not a gap.
#
# THE BETTER END STATE IS ONE AGGREGATE JOB. A `ci-ok` job that `needs:` every
# gating job and fails on failure/cancelled/skipped would let the matrix be
# renamed, split or made conditional without ever stranding a merge, and this
# file would name it alone. It is not added here because adding it is a CI
# change with its own review; the list below is what CI produces today.
[checks]
	required = fmt
	required = qemu-validation
	required = test / ubuntu-latest
	required = test / macos-latest
	required = test / windows-latest
	required = test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu
	required = test (release)
